  <title>1.9.4: Solving for a Specified Variable</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00211</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.9.4: Solving for a Specified Variable</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c5654359-cfc3-4341-9aa3-e49fcff77931</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Cool Down Activity </title>
      <para>A school is holding a carnival and hopes to raise $500. Child tickets cost $3, and adult
        tickets cost $5. If the school sells \( x \) child tickets and \( y \) adult tickets, then
        the equation \( 3x + 5y = 500 \) expresses the fact that the school raised exactly $500 from
        ticket sales.</para>
      
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Solve the equation for \( x \). </para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution> <para>Compare your answer:   \( x= \frac{500-5y}{3} \) </para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>

      

      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Explain when it might be helpful to rewrite the equation this way.</para>
          <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
        </problem>
        <solution> 
          <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here
          is a sample. Solving for \(x\) helps us find out the number of child tickets to sell if we
          know the number of adults attending the carnival and we want to make exactly $500 from
          ticket sales. </para>

        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
